\newlabel{fig:aggchoice}{{5}{22}{The choice of aggregate determines the conclusion. A flat mean over the four components hides the refusal-calibration effect almost entirely for Qwen2.5-32B, while CATS-Prevalence and CATS-Balanced both surface it. This is why we report components rather than a single number}{figure.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Refusal credit inside the aggregate. Left: CATS-Prevalence split into the part earned on answerable examples and the part earned on correct refusals, which are scored on their decision alone. Right: plotting the answerable-only score against the headline places every trace-tuned point below the diagonal, showing the headline is inflated by decision credit.}}{23}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:refcredit}{{6}{23}{Refusal credit inside the aggregate. Left: CATS-Prevalence split into the part earned on answerable examples and the part earned on correct refusals, which are scored on their decision alone. Right: plotting the answerable-only score against the headline places every trace-tuned point below the diagonal, showing the headline is inflated by decision credit}{figure.6}{}}
